# frozen_string_literal: true

source "https://rubygems.org"

gem "ruby-oci8"

# bigdecimal stopped being a default gem in Ruby 3.4.0, so anything that reaches
# it now has to declare it. Two separate routes get there, and both matter:
#
# - pg, mysql2 and trilogy emit `require "bigdecimal/util"` in the *generated
#   code* whenever a query applies `.to_d` to a decimal column
#   (crates/scythe-codegen/src/backends/ruby_rbs.rs,
#   ruby_generated_code_needs_bigdecimal_util). That gate is per generated file,
#   not per project, so it cannot be mirrored here -- declare the dependency
#   whenever the driver is capable of emitting it, rather than trying to know in
#   advance whether this project's schema has a decimal column.
#
# - oci8 emits no such require, but the *driver itself* does: ruby-oci8's
#   lib/oci8/bindtype.rb requires bigdecimal lazily when it decodes an Oracle
#   NUMBER column, and does not declare the dependency in its gemspec. Reading
#   any numeric column therefore raises LoadError on Ruby 3.4 with a bundle that
#   has not declared it. Gating on "does scythe emit the require" missed this
#   entirely -- the first CI run of ruby-oci8-oracle failed on exactly that.
gem "bigdecimal"
